To get optimal throughput, the latency needs to be as short as possible. To increase performance, assuming the loop bounds are static, the loop can be fully unrolled using the UNROLL pragma to create parallel implementations of the loop body. After the LOOP_1 is fully unrolled a significant reduction in the latency (50 ns) is shown in the following figure. Unrolling loops implies a trade-off by achieving higher performance but at the cost of using extra resources (as seen below in the increase of FFs and LUTs). Fully unrolling the loop causes the loop itself to disappear and be replaced by the parallel implementations of the loop body which uses up the extra resources as shown below.

Figure 21:  Performance & Resource Estimates

